  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure will be situated south of the \nregion with mid and upper level heights falling, as a cold front\napproaches to open the week. The mid-level flow becomes zonal \nwith the strong ridge over the Southeast and Gulf weakening over\nthe Northeast. Near ideal radiational cooling conditions are \nexpected tonight with light to calm winds and initially clear \nskies before some high clouds increase well after midnight. Lows\nwill be in the upper 20s to mid 30s, as we tried to blend the \nNBM and MAVMOS guidance. Some patchy mist/fog may form in the \nHudson/Mohawk River Valleys again, as the this morning, and near\nthe CT River Valley near southern VT. Tomorrow, a cold front \nwith limited low-level moisture moves across the region in the \nafternoon/early evening. A few showers are possible over the \nwest/southwest Adirondacks with light rainfall amounts under a \ntenth of an inch. This front will bring an increase in clouds \nand breezy conditions with gusts from the northwest mainly 15 to\n20 mph, though a few isolated gusts to 25 mph are possible. Max\ntemps will be above normal tomorrow in the mid 50s to mid/upper\n60s. A colder and drier air mass builds back in over the region\nMon night into Tue. High temps on Tue will run below normal in \nthe mid 40s to mid 50s with lighter winds, as another strong \nCanadian surface anticyclone builds in. \n\nIdeal radiational coolings are likely Tue night with cold temps with \nlows in the 20s to lower 30s.  The air mass begins to modify by the \nmid week, as the sfc high shifts offshore, as temps trend to \nseasonable readings. The mid and upper level flow becomes zonal Wed \nnight into Thu, as a warm front associated predominately with clouds \nmoves through and another mainly moisture starved dry cold front \npushes through Thu night thru Fri.  Temps rise above normal to close \nthe week. A few light rain showers may impact the locations \nwest/northwest of the Capital Region.  Next weekend looks to open \ndry with near to slightly above normal temps to close October. Many \nlocations be close to two weeks without measurable rainfall.",
